Issues.Body:
### Description

In .NET 10, a WPF app can no longer put an Enhanced Metafile on the clipboard for other applications. The format is advertised, but every request for it fails, so pasting into Word (e.g. Paste Special → Picture (Enhanced Metafile)) does nothing. The same code works in .NET 9.

This appears to have come in when WPF moved onto the shared OLE implementation in `System.Private.Windows.Core`.

### Analysis

1. `Composition.AllowedTymeds` is `TYMED_HGLOBAL | TYMED_ISTREAM | TYMED_GDI`. `ManagedToNativeAdapter.GetData` / `GetDataHere` / `QueryGetData` return `DV_E_TYMED` when `(tymed & AllowedTymeds) == 0`. `CF_ENHMETAFILE` is always requested as `TYMED_ENHMF`, so the request is rejected before the data object is consulted.
   - WPF on .NET 9 used `ALLOWED_TYMEDS = { HGLOBAL, ISTREAM, ENHMF, MFPICT, GDI }`. The shared mask matches what WinForms historically supported, which drops a capability WPF had.
2. The format enumerator still advertises `EnhancedMetafile` with `TYMED_ENHMF`, so consumers are offered a format and medium that `GetData` then refuses.
3. `WpfOleServices.GetDataHere` still has a `TYMED_ENHMF` branch, but it can no longer be reached. It also looks broken: for a `MemoryStream` it calls `SetEnhMetaFileBits` and checks the result, but never assigns the handle to `pmedium->u.hEnhMetaFile`, so it would return `S_OK` with a null handle even if reached. (.NET 9's `GetEnhancedMetafileHandle` returned the handle and the caller assigned it.)

### Reproduction Steps

WPF app, UI (STA) thread:

```csharp
const uint CF_ENHMETAFILE = 14;

// Any valid EMF. Note that WPF reads it with MemoryStream.GetBuffer(), so the stream must be exposable.
var stream = new MemoryStream();
stream.Write(CreateEmfBytes());
Clipboard.SetDataObject(new DataObject(DataFormats.EnhancedMetafile, stream), copy: false);

// Read it back the way another application would
OpenClipboard(IntPtr.Zero);
bool listed = IsClipboardFormatAvailable(CF_ENHMETAFILE); // true on both runtimes
IntPtr hemf = GetClipboardData(CF_ENHMETAFILE);           // .NET 9: valid handle; .NET 10: IntPtr.Zero
CloseClipboard();

static byte[] CreateEmfBytes()
{
	IntPtr hdc = CreateEnhMetaFileW(IntPtr.Zero, null, IntPtr.Zero, null);
	Rectangle(hdc, 0, 0, 200, 100);
	IntPtr hemf = CloseEnhMetaFile(hdc);
	var bytes = new byte[GetEnhMetaFileBits(hemf, 0, null)];
	GetEnhMetaFileBits(hemf, (uint)bytes.Length, bytes);
	DeleteEnhMetaFile(hemf);
	return bytes;
}
```

(P/Invoke declarations omitted: `user32` `OpenClipboard`, `CloseClipboard`, `IsClipboardFormatAvailable`, `GetClipboardData`; `gdi32` `CreateEnhMetaFileW`, `Rectangle`, `CloseEnhMetaFile`, `GetEnhMetaFileBits`, `DeleteEnhMetaFile`.)

Same app, multi-targeted, Windows 11 (10.0.26100):

| Scenario | .NET 9.0.20 | .NET 10.0.12 |
|---|---|---|
| EMF, delayed rendering (`copy: false`), read with `GetClipboardData` | ✅ valid EMF handle | ❌ format listed, `GetClipboardData` returns null |
| `OleGetClipboard` → `IDataObject::GetData(CF_ENHMETAFILE, TYMED_ENHMF)` | ✅ `S_OK` | ❌ `CLIPBRD_E_BAD_DATA` (0x800401D3) |
| EMF, flushed (`copy: true`) | ✅ valid EMF handle | ❌ format listed, no data |
| `Bitmap` (control) | ✅ | ✅ |

### Expected behavior

As in .NET 9: an EMF (as a `Metafile` or an exposable `MemoryStream`) placed on the clipboard through WPF can be pasted into other applications as `CF_ENHMETAFILE`.

### Actual behavior

Silent failure. There's no exception and the format is still advertised, but the data can't be retrieved, so EMF paste into other apps (e.g. Word's Paste Special → Picture) does nothing. Apps that fall back to another format get a lower-quality image instead of vector graphics.

### Regression?

YES

### Known Workarounds

_No response_

### Impact

- **Radius:** WPF apps on .NET 10 that put `EnhancedMetafile` on the clipboard, whether rendered later or up front. Other formats in the same data object still work. Metafile data, `MetaFilePict`, and EMF drag-and-drop go through the same allowed-type check and are very likely affected too (inferred from source, not tested). WinForms is unaffected, since it never offered EMF.
- **Reach:** Confirmed broken on 10.0.12 and working on 9.0.20. Very likely every .NET 10 release, since the allowed-type list comes from the `release/10.0` branch (still present on `main` as well). Any WPF app moving to .NET 10 that copies EMF hits this with no code changes.

